    "summary": "Dark Star, The Other One > Dark Star and rare Born Cross‑Eyed > Spanish Jam",
    "blurb": "The set is anchored by a scorching Dark Star and an intense The Other One > Dark Star sequence, highlighted further by the rare Born Cross‑Eyed > Spanish Jam pairing. Jerry’s solos are fiery throughout, especially on Morning Dew, while Pigpen’s organ is largely muted. Overall the show delivers high‑energy jamming with a few uneven transitions.",
    "review": "On March 30, 1968 at San Francisco's Carousel Ballroom the Dead were in full early‑Dead mode: raw, exploratory, and unafraid to stretch into the unknown. The first set opens with a luminous Morning Dew that showcases Jerry’s lyrical phrasing before diving into the classic “Cryptical Envelopment” sandwich framing a volatile The Other One leading back into Cryptical. Dark Star erupts next, feeding directly into a bright China Cat Sunflower → The Eleven segue that, while not perfectly smooth, still radiates the band’s collective drive. \nThe second set is where the night truly shines – a rare Born Cross‑Eyed → Feedback → Spanish Jam stretch that captures the group’s willingness to experiment, followed by a soulful Death Don't Have No Mercy and an exuberant turn on your Lovelight. Throughout the evening Jerry delivers searing leads on Dark Star, The Other One, and Morning Dew; Pigpen’s keyboard is unfortunately muted for much of the night, leaving the rhythm section to carry the groove with solid work from Phil, Mickey, and Bill. Despite some rough transitions, the show remains a compelling snapshot of the Dead’s evolving improvisational language.",
